  real `ifstream` against actual empty/whitespace-only/garbage files) and found the real rule has
  two branches: if the stream hits EOF *before finding any non-whitespace byte* (empty file,
  whitespace-only file), the `sentry` itself fails without ever touching `value`, leaving it at
  the caller's `-1` pre-init; only once a non-whitespace byte *is* found but doesn't parse into a
  valid integer does `num_get::get` run and set `value = 0`. The original code's `if !saw_digit {
  return 0 }` fired identically for both cases, so an empty or whitespace-only sysfs file read as
  `0` instead of the correct `-1` — contradicted by its own doc comment and locked in by a test
  that asserted the wrong value. The review also caught a same-root-cause companion bug: the
  leading-whitespace-skip loop used `is_ascii_whitespace()` instead of the module's own
  hand-rolled `C_ISSPACE` (which the module's *own* doc comment already explained is necessary
  because Rust's ASCII-whitespace set omits vertical tab, unlike C's `isspace`) — so a value like
  `"\v42"` would fail to skip the leading `\v` and misparse. Both fixed: `read_sysfs_int` now
  detects "no non-whitespace byte before EOF" as its own branch (returns `-1`, matching the
  sentry-failure path) separately from "found a byte, couldn't parse it" (returns `0`), and uses
  `C_ISSPACE` for the leading skip. Doc comment corrected to describe the real two-branch rule
  (noting it was verified against real `istream`/`ifstream` behavior, not just recited). Test
  fixed (empty file now expects `-1`) and extended with a whitespace-only-file case (also `-1`), a
