## 2026-07-31 (session 62)
Done: task 5.6.5.3 (dlopen'd vendor GPU libraries — `NvidiaNvmlReader`/`AmdRsmiReader`). New
  `crates/noctalia-system/src/dl.rs` — a shared `dlopen`/`dlsym`/`dlclose` wrapper (`DlLibrary`)
  plus a `SymbolSource` trait that separates "which symbol name resolves" (pure, cascade logic:
  `resolve_symbol_name`) from "fetch the pointer" (real `dlsym`), so the preferred/fallback name
  cascade is unit-testable against a fake without a real `.so` — this is the "dlsym-loading logic
  structured to be testable" seam the task's own done-bar calls for. New
  `crates/noctalia-system/src/gpu_nvml.rs` (`NvidiaNvmlReader`: single `libnvidia-ml.so.1` dlopen,
  7 dlsym'd NVML symbols with preferred/fallback names, `read_gpu_temp_sensor`/
  `read_gpu_usage_percent`/`read_gpu_vram` — all three do a full, no-short-circuit scan across every
  device, matching the C++ exactly, unlike RSMI below) and `crates/noctalia-system/src/gpu_rsmi.rs`
  (`AmdRsmiReader`: 6-candidate `librocm_smi64.so{,.5,.6,.7,.1.0}` dlopen cascade, ~13 dlsym'd RSMI
  symbols, the v1-major-version disambiguation via probing `rsmi_dev_activity_metric_get`'s
  presence to pick the v5-vs-v6 `rsmi_dev_gpu_clk_freq_get` struct layout, `ready`/
  `read_temp_sensor`/`read_usage` — both early-return on the first device that succeeds, matching
  the C++'s loop shape, unlike NVML's full-scan). Both readers reuse `TempSensorReading`/
  `GpuVramReading`/`SysfsGpuUsageReading`/`has_usable_vram` from task 5.6.5.2's `gpu_sysfs.rs`
  rather than redefining them.
  The RSMI reader's per-device init-time probe loop (`rsmi_dev_name_get`/`rsmi_dev_power_cap_get`/
  `rsmi_dev_temp_metric_get(max)`, results discarded) is ported faithfully even though nothing
  reads the results afterward — matches a real oddity in the C++ itself (confirmed by direct
  reading: `AmdRsmiReader::ensureReady` calls these three per device and discards every result).
  Several RSMI symbols (`rsmi_dev_memory_busy_percent_get`, `rsmi_dev_power_ave_get`,
  `rsmi_dev_memory_total_get`, `rsmi_dev_memory_usage_get`, `rsmi_dev_pci_throughput_get`,
  `rsmi_dev_gpu_clk_freq_get`) are loaded (their absence still fails `ensure_ready`, matching the
  C++'s all-or-nothing `loadDlsymFunction` chain) but not kept as struct fields since the C++ never
  reads them back either beyond that one load-time validation — a deliberate simplification to
  avoid dead Rust fields with no behavioral difference from the C++'s unused member-field storage,
  not a shortcut on the *gating* behavior itself.
  `Drop` on both readers' session structs calls `shutdown` before the library's own `Drop`
  `dlclose`s it (Rust's guaranteed drop order: custom `Drop::drop` body runs before field auto-drop
  in declaration order), matching the C++'s explicit `close()` (shutdown-then-dlclose) sequencing
  without needing an explicit `close()` method; early-init-failure paths call `shutdown` manually
  then rely on the local `DlLibrary` binding's own `Drop` to `dlclose` on function return.
  No C++ test exists for these readers in isolation (`system_monitor_service_test.cpp` runs with
  `gpuPollSeconds = 0`) — task 5.6's own done bar is fixture/seam-driven tests for the dlsym-
